---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Tool reference | Milton"
  description="Milton's MCP connector: setup, permissions, its two tools with their inputs and outputs, side effects, errors and limits."
>
  <article class="briefing">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">## Tool reference</p>
      <h1>Milton’s two tools</h1>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="overview">
      <h2 id="overview">What the connector does</h2>
      <p>
        Milton keeps what you wrote and what you changed. Your AI reads it
        before it drafts. Each memo holds an AI’s first draft and the final the
        person accepted; Milton’s code works out the person’s tweaks, the
        changes they made to the draft. No AI writes them, and Milton keeps no
        standing rules. Reads and saves make no model call. The connector sends,
        buys and publishes nothing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="setup">
      <h2 id="setup">Setup and permissions</h2>
      <p>
        MCP server: <code class="mcp-url">https://mcp.meetmilton.com/mcp</code>,
        over Streamable HTTP. Sign in with OAuth (email and password, Google or
        GitHub); clients that can’t open a browser use a connection key from the
        app’s Connected AIs page as a bearer token. Step-by-step guides for each
        AI are on <a href="/setup">Setup</a>.
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Read only</strong> (<code>milton:read</code>): lists{" "}
          <code>get_report</code> alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read and write</strong> (<code>milton:read</code> and{" "}
          <code>milton:write</code>): also lists <code>create_memo</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The person chooses on the consent screen, and the server enforces it. A
        Milton account is required: a 30-day free trial with no card, then $5.01
        USD a month.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="get-report">
      <h2 id="get-report">
        <code>get_report</code>: read
      </h2>
      <p>
        Read-only, not sensitive. Called at the start of writing work, and when
        the person asks “Did you get the memo?”. Returns the report for one
        draft.
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Input:</strong> <code>intent</code> with{" "}
          <code>situation</code> (one line: what is being written, to whom and
          why), <code>medium</code> (email, chat, post, document or speech),{" "}
          <code>audience</code>, <code>purpose</code>, and an optional{" "}
          <code>kind_id</code> from the person’s kinds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Output:</strong> <code>text</code>, the person’s best-matching
          finals as examples, best first, with what they changed in the AI’s
          first draft under drafted ones (quoted as historical text, not
          instructions); <code>examples</code> (memo ids and context, in served
          order); <code>kinds</code>; <code>is_empty</code>; <code>memos</code>,
          the person’s memo count; and <code>keep_memos</code>. While the count
          is 0, a read-and-write connection also gets <code>setup</code>, the
          first-setup script; a read-only one gets a permission line instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Side effects:</strong> none on the person’s writing. Milton
          counts the call for usage.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="create-memo">
      <h2 id="create-memo">
        <code>create_memo</code>: write
      </h2>
      <p>
        Write, not sensitive. Saves one memo when the person accepts a final,
        forgets memos they name, or corrects a memo’s context. Each call sends{" "}
        <code>request_id</code> (any new id, 1 to 64 letters, digits, dots,
        dashes, underscores or colons) and exactly one of:
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <code>memo</code>: <code>final</code>, the accepted text exactly;{" "}
          <code>context</code> (a one-line <code>summary</code>,{" "}
          <code>medium</code>, and optional <code>audience</code> and{" "}
          <code>purpose</code>); and, for a drafted memo,{" "}
          <code>first_draft</code> as first shown (or{" "}
          <code>first_draft_missing</code>) with <code>mode</code> (
          <code>draft</code> or <code>edit</code>) and an optional{" "}
          <code>request</code>, what the person asked for, in their own words,
          or one short line when they only agreed. A <code>kind_id</code> or{" "}
          <code>new_kind</code> files it. Writing the person gives to keep sends
          only final, context and kind.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>forget_memos</code>: 1 to 100 memo ids the person asked to
          forget.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>memo_context</code>: a memo id with its corrected context or
          kind; a memo’s words never change.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A save returns <code>status</code> (<code>saved</code> or{" "}
        <code>unchanged</code>), the memo’s id, tier and kind, the changes
        Milton found, and <code>receipt</code>, one line for the AI to repeat,
        such as “Kept memo: vendor decline.” Milton stores the final and first
        draft exactly as sent; a save is all or nothing. Repeating a call with
        the same <code>request_id</code> and arguments returns its original
        outcome and never saves twice; the same id with other arguments is
        refused. Forgetting deletes the memo and its tweaks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="errors">
      <h2 id="errors">Errors and next steps</h2>
      <p>
        Every error carries <code>error</code>, a <code>message</code>, a stable{" "}
        <code>code</code> and <code>next</code>, whose <code>action</code> is
        one of:
      </p>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <code>retry</code>: repeat the identical call, a save with its{" "}
          <code>request_id</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>reread</code>: read the report again, then save with a new{" "}
          <code>request_id</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>fix_input</code>: correct the arguments as the message says.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>ask_user</code>: the fix needs the person’s approval or choice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>tell_user</code>: stop and tell the person; retrying won’t help
          (for example a read-only connection, or a paused subscription).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Fields and tools earlier versions took (<code>get_cover_sheet</code>, a
        cover sheet, a memo’s tweaks, rounds or report id) are refused with
        guidance, never accepted silently.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="limits">
      <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>A request body of 256 KiB; a final or first draft of 256 KiB.</li>
        <li>
          A summary of 200 characters; an audience or purpose of 100; a kind
          label of 60; a request of 600 characters and 12 lines.
        </li>
        <li>
          A report’s examples fit about 8,000 tokens, with at most three AI
          drafts the person steered and none they accepted untouched.
        </li>
        <li>
          Connection-key lookups are rate limited per network; there is no daily
          call or save allowance.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Disconnecting an AI in Milton blocks its next request. Its memos stay
        with the person, who can forget them, delete them all or delete the
        account in the app. Questions: <a href="/support">Support</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>

